# DEPRECATED - this file no longer contains a certificate.
#
# Keploy used to ship one static MITM certificate authority here, and this URL
# is still referenced by old Dockerfiles that baked it into an image:
#
#   ADD https://raw.githubusercontent.com/keploy/keploy/refs/heads/main/pkg/core/proxy/tls/asset/ca.crt ...
#
# That static CA has been retired because its private key was committed to this
# public repository. Keploy now generates a fresh MITM CA for every run and
# removes it when the run ends, so there is nothing to bake in.
#
# You do NOT need to add any certificate to your image. Since keploy v3.3.0 the
# tooling injects its per-run CA and the matching trust environment variables
# (SSL_CERT_FILE, NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS, REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE, JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS,
# ...) into your application container automatically for `keploy record`/`test`
# with docker run, docker compose and --from-container.
#
# To remove the old static CA that a previous keploy version may have left
# trusted on a host, run:  sudo keploy ca clean --fresh
#
# See https://keploy.io/docs/running-keploy/docker-tls/ for the current setup.
